# Do Not Open Your Mouth
Tamil title: வாய் திறவாதீர்

Let the swan-down couch be splendidly decorated!

Let the arati and ceremonial fans be made ready near the ritual entrance!

Have the voices of cuckoos assembled in the audience hall?

Go and see whether the dancing beauties have adorned themselves well.

The hour grows late. Let the great drum sound!

Let masters of the nine sentiments gather in our court!

Are the drinks ready? Where are the delicacies? Are the silken garments prepared?

Summon the herald. Carry the perfumes in procession. Where is the royal horse? [The Maharani]

“The King of Kings, Sun among Kings, Lord of Kings, the Maharaja of Darbhanga, enters the royal court. Attention! Attention! Attention!” [The herald]

“Maharaja of Maharajas, I bless you. May auspiciousness prevail. Be seated upon the throne.” [The palace priest]

“Maharaja, we bow, we bow.” [The chief ministers]

“Let the dance begin and our Maharaja behold it with delight.” [A minister]

“Who in the world is his equal? Who is a lion of victorious valour like him? Tell us!” [The dancing women]

“Ladies, bow to the king and leave. Our ruler has grown weary from watching the dance.” [A minister]

“Our salutations! Our salutations! Our salutations!” [The dancing women]

Maharaja! In your palace, in your abode of bliss and hall of power, magnificent speeches and festive celebrations must have occurred just as I have depicted above. They must have waved the arati to ward off the evil eye. Once the other pleasures ended, surrounded by lovely women in the inner apartments, you must have approached the swan-down couch. Need anything more be said?

The priest tells you that your life, immersed in sensual pleasure, was obtained through merit accumulated in a former birth. The ministers declare that the king's command is surely God's command. What made me think today of you, who lead such a life of serene amusement, was the fact that you had deigned to open your sacred mouth.

People such as you have found a way to live amid playful diversions. Once a man becomes a ruler, whether or not he knows how to govern and whether or not he possesses intelligence, can the people refuse to say that the king rules? Even if he shouts in madness, they do not hesitate to call it divine song. Scoundrels say that Maheswara created the people solely to endure the pride, foolishness and cruelty of kings.

Under that arrangement, I have no concern over what kind of life you lead. You have been king of kings not merely since yesterday, but for a very long time. Throughout those days I neither spoke nor thought about you. What compelled me to write today was your speech.

Maharaja of Darbhanga! Kings of former times are said to have possessed four kinds of army and to have been tigers in battle. Those fearless men attacked forts and crossed moats. That was once. Today there is no army, though there is never a shortage of doe-eyed women. This is the age of rulers whose warfare is with sinful Cupid and who fall before women's smiles. Nor is there now any work of fighting enemies or suppressing forest tribes, hill people and robbers. Most disturbances in today's palaces probably concern only the inner apartments.

“Is she some celestial Rambha? That night, as you embraced me, showed me the moon, stroked my forehead and bit my lip in pleasure, did you not swear upon our family deity that you would never think of another even in a dream? Yet now you went secretly to sport with that short little woman. Is your heart stone?

“Am I a grey-haired hag? Is there no brightness in my eyes? Have my nerves gone slack? In what way is that dancing Nagavalli superior to me?

“Is a maid your proper partner? You dallied with the woman who came to wash my sari. Is that fitting? Have you no honour? It is true that lust is blind. While I, the crowned queen, remain, you submitted to a servant in the bedchamber. Are you a sovereign?”

Perhaps royal women ask such questions. Or perhaps kings ask their wives:

“Hey, Madana! Why does that ministerial fellow come to meet you in the flower garden each evening?

“What business had the court musician in the inner apartments at midnight? Was that the hour for music? The guard says that he sang while you lay upon his lap. Is it true?

“I heard your coaxing words and was ruined, you deceiving woman! What business had you with the commander in the ruined hall at midnight?”

What quarrels do today's kings of kings possess other than disturbances of this sort? Why do they need a fourfold army? For midnight battles in which eyes meet, deceivers carry messages, and graceful women writhe, why elephants or armies?

The British army provides protection, and the priestly army bewilders the people with talk of sin and merit. Are not the kings of our subcontinent handsome men who rule without worry or fear? Even actors playing kings at midnight fear that the crown may fall, the pasted moustache detach, or the sword snap while they run. Real kings have not even those worries.

When you, a member of this carefree company, come forward to speak on public affairs, everyone who understands the matter will clap and laugh just as I do.

A few days ago, twirling your moustache, you presided over the All-India Landholders' Conference. You threatened that landlordism has existed since ancient times and that disturbing it would destroy the social order. When an owl in its hollow, a fox hidden in scrub, or a snake in its mound fears danger to its dwelling, it naturally hisses with all its force. In the same way, fearing danger to the courts of people like yourself, you hiss and threaten.

Would expelling bats from a mansion endanger the mansion? Likewise, what harm will befall society if landlord domination disappears? You said the social order would change. True. But what loss is there if it changes? Which order will change?

On one side are cultivators who rise with the cock, enter the mud, labour like cattle, and languish in lives surrounded by misery. On the other are grandees who sell the red rice those cultivators grow and harvest, turn it into capital, and use it for their amusements. Anxiety stands on one side and entertainment on the other. Wealth swells in mansions while people sob in huts. The landlord passes his days with doe-eyed women, horse races, goat fights, cockfights, dance, song and drink. The cultivator sees his wife, carrying a pot of millet gruel upon her head, give birth beside the field, and cries out to the gods of field, grove, threshing floor and tank bank.

This present social order, filled with injustice, wrongdoing and obscenity, will change. It certainly must. Only then can the people find comfort and everyone enjoy life's happiness.

King, do you, living a life of indolent luxury in a palace, know the poor cultivator's suffering? Do you know that he never eats his fill? Is it just that he should drown and wither in poverty? Do you know that he cannot obtain even one-thousandth of the pleasure enjoyed by landlords such as you who profit from him? Knowing this, do you still condemn the uprising that would raise his condition? What hardness of heart! Did God create the iron strongbox and the human heart without any connection between them?

Hear one thing. The age of those who live elegantly and pleasurably is ending. The age in which the worker demands rights is dawning. Before that sunrise neither arrogance nor authority will endure. The number who bow to courtly pronouncements will diminish. This law cannot be changed. No one will any longer respect the argument, “Would you alter what has existed so long?” Understand this. If you do not, I do not care. The pace of time is no gentle thing; those who study history thoughtfully know it.

I hear you thundered that communism must be abolished while speaking in the decorated hall arranged for you. You would not, and could not, speak so in a people's assembly. So long as an arrangement exists in which the majority must labour merely so a few may roll in luxury, a communist uprising must exist. How can it be abolished?

Maharaja, with the obedient servants who kneel before you, you may suppress the people's uprising for a while. You may fill prisons and construct gallows. But the fire will spread. It cannot be extinguished. It will destroy palaces and burn arrogance to ash.

You are a king through British favour. The Tsar was not. He was Russia's warrior and commander, beloved of the priests. At the movement of his little finger armies marched; at a glance from him armed guards assembled. Authority was his birthright. What was his fate? He too roared that the social system, economic arrangement and political order must not change by an atom. What happened?

But how can kings possess the condition needed to understand that? Residents of harems have no reason to acquire historical clarity. That class therefore lives upon the fantasy that verbal threats can bend the world. They understand nothing until fire spreads in all directions. History says that even while Rome burned, Nero calmly played his lyre.

You said sorrowfully that by imposing many new taxes the government would ruin the position of hereditary landholders. Higher taxes may require you to reduce your drinks, bedroom ornamentation, and ostentatious expenditure a little. You raise a great cry even over that. Do you know the cruelty of the taxes imposed upon the poor, enough to bend their entire lives? Have you seen their sweat and tears flow mingled together?

Ruler of the Darbhanga estate, should not a civilised government place the tax burden upon those able to bear it and ensure that none rests upon those already staggering? You dare oppose this only because you lie exhausted inside the palace, where the poor people's suffering cannot reach your eyes.

I hear you also said the country must not be divided. Excellent, excellent, king! Publish your noble counsel when the people request it. The question of dividing the country is not one you can understand. It belongs to people struggling with life; maharajas cannot comprehend it properly.

You presided over the All-India Landholders' Conference without knowing the condition of people fallen into poverty. Your conference met in Calcutta, where thousands starved and became corpses beside the road; where people reduced to skin and bone lay half-alive as dogs and foxes tore at them; where heaps of bodies lay beside drains and in the middle of streets; where people died crying, “Hunger! Hunger! Hunger!”

Even after gathering in that Calcutta, you could find no way for the people to recover. What can you do? What can the king of kings know of the people's condition?

O righteous advocate for landlords, do not incur the anger of the crores in this country who languish without knowing a peaceful life. See to enjoying your pageantry while it lasts, and then depart. Do not stab an open wound. Do not open your mouth. Our stomachs burn.

**3 September 1944**

---NOTES---
The article is a polemical open letter to the Maharaja of Darbhanga following his address to the All-India Landholders' Conference. Its theatrical opening imitates an extravagant royal court before turning to criticism of landlordism, princely privilege, taxation and opposition to social change.

`Amsa-thulika manjam` denotes a luxurious couch filled with swan down. `Alavattam` is a ceremonial fan, and the `parakku` herald loudly announces a ruler's arrival. The fourfold army traditionally comprises infantry, cavalry, elephants and chariots. `Rambha` is a celestial dancer; `Madana` is the god of love. `Kambankoozh` is a porridge made from pearl millet.

The source contains severe OCR damage in the transition introducing the demand to abolish communism. The surrounding legible sentences make that subject clear, and it is translated accordingly without altering the verbatim Tamil source. The claims about the Darbhanga ruler, Calcutta famine, Tsar and Nero are retained as the article's rhetoric and have not been independently verified here.
